n urgent need for a  transplant  morgan campbell global news toronto  chugging on nearly a century on up next  a club's hopes to keep its legacy on the  rails  whether on foot or wheels competitors  were back on track as the boston  marathon finally resumed after a  30-month hiatus the world famous race  had been put on hold by the pandemic  coveted measures were still in place  racers had to be vaccinated or show a  negative test and bystanders were asked  not to offer any kisses for good luck  it seems everybody has a hobby these  days whether it's honing a valuable  skill or just passing the time  but not everyone thinks of leaving a  lasting legacy with it that's certainly  the case for the burnaby central  railways engineers in bc who have kept  their model trains chugging for nearly a  hundred years as global bc's jay durant  found out the club is working hard to  recruit new members to keep its future  on track  [Music]  it is one of the oldest most successful  miniature railway clubs in the world its  origins date back to 1929  and volunteers have now been running  trains for the public since the mid 70s  lawyers architects teachers engineers  all drawn by the social aspect of the  club and to satisfy their mechanical  itch by building and fixing trains  we have three generations of our family  here at the park myself  our son  and our grandchildren now but they've  been on a recruiting mission in recent  years trying to attract new members it  hasn't been easy but they do have some  fresh faces learning from the veterans i  was at a train expo while back at p e  and then joe came by i was like oh we  want some uh young blood and young  members to help  bring down the average age there's a lot  of history for them to absorb the club  needed more room to stretch its tracks  so in the early 90s it moved to its  current location at confederation park  in burnaby construction coincided with  the digging of the cassier tunnel a  short distance away and all that earth  was put to good use we got all the fill  from there built it up and they did a  really good job of doing a very  big track layout to fit into the little  space that we have here  now they're working to keep the history  alive mentoring the new club members  a lot of people with a lot of skills  fixing our steam engines we repair them  on site for the most part and we need to  pass that on to generations at skill  because it's going to be lost otherwise  right in there and they're picking it up  quickly training for what it takes to  keep these engines rolling do it on the  flat surfaces down here  i'm learning new skills like wiring  mechanics  metal work would work  the future still looks good for a club  approaching its centennial anniversary  there's a big age gap out here but the  new recruits are very enthusiastic and  eager to learn from the senior members  they just have to put up with all the  dad jokes what's the worst one you've  heard  um  [Music]  i cannot think i've heard so many bad  ones here  jay durant global news  and that is global national for this  monday i'm farah nasser and tonight's  your canada is the stunning fall colors  in montreal quebec thank you for  watching and have a happy thanksgiving  donna friesen is back at the anchor desk  tomorrow take care of yourselves and  each other  [Music]  you
 

